<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hybrid reward consistently improves performance across all regimes.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hybrid Reinforcement:When Reward Is Sparse, It’s Better to Be Dense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> outperforms both reward-model-only and rule-based verifier baselines across all three regimes—verifiable, hard-to-verify, and mixed. On verifiable tasks, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> achieves an average of 62.0, exceeding RM-only (56.4) and rule-based training (58.3). The improvement comes from stratified normalization, which allows hybrid training to exploit both positive and negative correctness groups: while verifier-only training collapses all-correct or all-incorrect batches (yielding zero relative advantage), <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> preserves meaningful gradients within each group through dense intra-group rewards. The benefit becomes most pronounced on hard-to-verify tasks, where symbolic verifiers are brittle and reward models are noisy. Here, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> attains 56.8, surpassing RM-only (54.1) by +2.7 points and rule-based verifiers (42.6) by a large +14.2 points. This gain stems from anchoring continuous reward-model scores within verifier correctness groups, which prevents reward drift and ensures stable learning even when binary labels saturate. By combining the verifier’s precision with the reward model’s smooth discrimination, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> can leverage partially correct reasoning paths that rule-based systems would discard, thereby improving both stability and coverage. In the mixed regime, which combines verifiable and open-ended samples, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> again achieves the best average (58.8), outperforming RM-only (55.0) and verifier-only (56.1). Overall, hybrid reward learning delivers consistent improvements across all settings while retaining the efficiency of reward-model supervision, demonstrating that structured reward integration is critical for reasoning tasks that go beyond strict verifiability. The advantage becomes even clearer on hard-to-verify evaluations (HVM, TBR), where symbolic verifiers fail to capture partial correctness and reward models are prone to drift. Here, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> attains 66.3 when trained on easy-to-verify data, outperforming RM-only (54.6) by +11.7 points and rule-based training (57.1) by +9.2 points. When trained on hard-to-verify samples, it still leads with 56.5 compared to 53.7 (RM-only) and 54.2 (rule-based), and under mixed training, it reaches 64.1, surpassing both baselines by large margins. These results highlight that hybrid reward design generalizes robustly across both verifiable and hard-to-verify tasks, yielding stable improvements regardless of whether evaluation relies on symbolic checking or model judgment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hybrid reward generalizes across backbones.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hybrid reward learning consistently scales across models with different capacities and initialization quality. On Qwen3-4B (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hybrid Reinforcement:When Reward Is Sparse, It’s Better to Be Dense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which already shows strong supervised performance, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> continues to yield clear improvements across all evaluation regimes, demonstrating that hybrid optimization remains effective even for well-trained reasoning models. More strikingly, on OctoThinker-8B (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Hybrid reward generalizes across backbones. ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hybrid Reinforcement:When Reward Is Sparse, It’s Better to Be Dense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which starts from a much weaker baseline of 16.9 on verifiable and 23.6 on hard-to-verify tasks, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HERO</span> achieves substantial absolute and relative gains. When trained on easy-to-verify samples, it reaches 40.1 on verifiable and 32.6 on hard-to-verify evaluations, surpassing both the reward-model baseline (38.1/28.7) and the rule-based verifier (37.6/30.3). Training on hard-to-verify samples yields similar improvements, achieving 41.0/34.6 compared to 35.4/30.9 (RM-only) and 34.6/27.8 (verifier-only). In the mixed regime, it maintains the highest averages of 40.2 and 33.2, outperforming all baselines by 4–6 points. These results show that hybrid reward design generalizes robustly across model scales—preserving the verifier’s stability for stronger models like Qwen3-4B while bringing large relative gains to weaker ones such as OctoThinker-8B.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
